In C, a **file** is simply a persistent stream of bytes stored outside the program’s memory (on disk, flash, network, etc.).  
C treats files as opaque objects that you manipulate through the standard I/O library (`stdio.h`).  

Below is a quick reference guide covering the essentials:

| Concept | What it means | Typical API |
|---------|---------------|-------------|
| **File object** | An abstract representation of an opened file. In C this is a `FILE *` pointer. | `FILE *fp;` |
| **Opening a file** | Allocate a `FILE *`, associate it with a pathname, and set its access mode. | `fopen(const char *pathname, const char *mode)` |
| **Closing a file** | Flush buffers, release resources, and invalidate the `FILE *`. | `fclose(FILE *stream)` |
| **Reading / writing** | Transfer data between your program and the file. | `fread(void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, FILE *stream)`<br>`fwrite(const void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, FILE *stream)` |
| **Seeking / telling** | Move the file position indicator or query it. | `fseek(FILE *stream, long offset, int whence)`<br>`ftell(FILE *stream)` |
| **Error handling** | Check return values and use `errno`/`perror()` for diagnostics. | `if (fp == NULL) perror("fopen failed");` |

### Modes for `fopen`

| Mode string | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| `"r"` | Read only, text mode, fail if file doesn’t exist. |
| `"w"` | Write only, text mode, truncate or create. |
| `"a"` | Append only, text mode, create if missing. |
| `"rb"` | Read only, binary mode. |
| `"wb"` | Write only, binary mode. |
| `"ab"` | Append only, binary mode. |
| `"r+"` | Read/write, text mode, fail if missing. |
| `"w+"` | Read/write, text mode, truncate or create. |
| `"a+"` | Read/append, text mode, create if missing. |
| `"r+b"` | Read/write, binary mode, fail if missing. |
| `"w+b"` | Read/write, binary mode, truncate or create. |
| `"a+b"` | Read/append, binary mode, create if missing. |

> **Tip:** On Windows, text mode translates `\n` ↔ `\r\n`; binary mode preserves raw bytes.

### Minimal Example – Reading a Text File

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = fopen("example.txt", "r");
    if (!fp) {
        perror("Failed to open file");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    char buffer[256];
    while (fgets(buffer, sizeof buffer, fp)) {   // reads one line at a time
        printf("%s", buffer);                    // prints the line
    }

    if (ferror(fp))
        perror("Error reading file");

    fclose(fp);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

### Writing Binary Data

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = fopen("data.bin", "wb");
    if (!fp) {
        perror("Cannot open file for writing");
        return 1;
    }

    uint32_t numbers[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};
    size_t written = fwrite(numbers, sizeof(uint32_t), 4, fp);
    if (written != 4)
        perror("Write error");

    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
```

### Common Pitfalls & Best Practices

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| Forgetting to close a file | Always call `fclose()` when finished. |
| Ignoring return values | Check `fopen()`, `fread()`, `fwrite()`, `fclose()` for errors. |
| Mixing text/binary modes incorrectly | Use `"b"` suffix on Windows; on POSIX it has no effect but keeps code portable. |
| Buffer overrun with `fgets()` | Pass the correct buffer size (`sizeof buffer`) to avoid overflow. |
| Not checking `feof()` before reading | `feof()` only becomes true after a failed read attempt; check the return value of `fread()/fgets()` instead. |

---

#### Quick Reference Code Snippet

```c
// Open file for reading
FILE *fp = fopen("myfile.txt", "r");
if (!fp) { perror("open"); exit(1); }

// Read entire file into a dynamically allocated buffer
fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END);
long size = ftell(fp);
rewind(fp);

char *content = malloc(size + 1);
if (!content) { perror("malloc"); fclose(fp); exit(1); }

size_t nread = fread(content, 1, size, fp);
content[nread] = '\0';          // null‑terminate for safety

printf("Read %zu bytes:\n%s\n", nread, content);

free(content);
fclose(fp);
```

